{%- comment -%} Canonical + noindex rules per Vol II §10.4. Include once in <head>. {%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Titles key off the brand setting, not shop.name. The Shopify account was
  named "Baby Products" when this was written — shop.name is what Shopify
  feeds page_title on the homepage, so reading it directly put the account
  name in every tab and every shared link. The account has since been renamed
  to MamaJoy, which makes the two agree today; the indirection stays because
  agreeing today is not the same as being the same field, and the store name
  is editable by anyone with admin access. Same fallback chain the header and
  footer already use.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign brand = settings.brand_name | default: shop.name -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Captured once and reused for og:title below. The homepage's page_title is
  the account name, which no amount of appending fixes — it has to be
  replaced outright — and og:title read page_title directly, which put the
  account name into every shared link's preview on the one template <title>
  was written to protect.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if template.name == 'index' -%}
  {%- capture doc_title -%}{{ brand }}{% if settings.brand_tagline != blank %} · {{ settings.brand_tagline }}{% endif %}{%- endcapture -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- capture doc_title -%}{{ page_title }}{% unless page_title contains brand %} · {{ brand }}{% endunless %}{%- endcapture -%}
{%- endif -%}
<title>{{ doc_title }}</title>
{%- if page_description -%}<meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Collections are no longer noindexed as a class. They were, and at the time
  that was right: every collection was either empty or a second listing of
  the same two mats, and a blanket rule was the only one that could not get
  it wrong.

  It is wrong now in one specific way — it also suppresses /collections/play-mats,
  which has products, has a hand-written SEO title and description, and is the
  page that should rank for the category term the whole catalog is built on.
  A store cannot rank for "baby play mats" while telling Google not to look at
  its baby play mats page.

  So the rule is earned rather than assumed: a collection is indexable when it
  has at least one product AND carries a hand-written SEO meta description.
  Both halves matter. Products alone would let /collections/all and
  /collections/new-arrivals in — same two mats, third and fourth URL, which is
  the thin-duplicate problem the blanket rule existed to prevent.

  The second half is specifically the global.description_tag metafield — the
  Search engine listing box in the admin — and not collection.description,
  which is the body copy shown on the page. Every collection in this store
  already has body copy, including all six empty ones, so testing that would
  admit new-arrivals on the strength of a line someone wrote to fill a page.
  The SEO field is the one nobody fills in by accident.

  This is also why the six empty collections need no special handling and no
  deletion. They stay live, stay linked-to when the merchant wants them, and
  stay out of the index on their own — and the day one is stocked and given a
  description, it becomes indexable with no code change. Empty is a state
  here, not a decision.

  noindex,follow rather than plain noindex throughout: the crawler is still
  wanted on the product links these pages carry.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign robots_noindex = false
  if template.name == 'page' and page.metafields.custom.lp_canonical_product != blank
    assign robots_noindex = true
  elsif template.name == 'search' or template.name == 'cart'
    assign robots_noindex = true
  elsif template.name == 'collection'
    assign robots_noindex = true
    assign collection_seo_desc = collection.metafields.global.description_tag
    if collection.products_count > 0 and collection_seo_desc != blank
      assign robots_noindex = false
    endif
  endif
-%}
{%- if template.name == 'page' and page.metafields.custom.lp_canonical_product != blank -%}
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ shop.url }}{{ page.metafields.custom.lp_canonical_product.value.url }}">
{%- else -%}
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
{%- endif -%}
{%- if robots_noindex -%}
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow">
{%- endif -%}

<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ brand | escape }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ doc_title | escape }}">
{%- if page_description -%}<meta property="og:description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">{%- endif -%}
<meta property="og:type" content="{% if template.name == 'product' %}product{% else %}website{% endif %}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical_url }}">
<meta property="og:locale" content="{{ request.locale.iso_code | replace: '-', '_' }}">

{%- comment -%}
  og:image, resolved in priority order rather than on the product template
  alone. Before this, every page that was not a PDP — the homepage most of
  all — shared with no image at all, and a link preview with no image is the
  one a person scrolls past. Each candidate is only used if it actually
  exists, so nothing here can emit a broken image URL:

    1. the product's own featured image, on a PDP
    2. the collection's image, on a collection page
    3. the page's own featured image, on a content page
    4. the brand's share image from Theme settings, anywhere
    5. the first product's own featured image, as the last resort that
       still shows what this shop actually sells

  Step 5 is what makes this hold on the homepage today, where no image of
  its own exists and no setting has been picked yet: the alternative was
  emitting nothing, which is the state this replaced. It is a catalog read
  in <head>, so it is deliberately last and only reached when the four
  cheaper candidates have all come back blank.

  1200px wide is the width both Facebook and X want for a large card, and
  Shopify's CDN negotiates the format per requesting client.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign og_img = blank
  if template.name == 'product' and product.featured_image != blank
    assign og_img = product.featured_image
  elsif template.name == 'collection' and collection.image != blank
    assign og_img = collection.image
  elsif template.name == 'page' and page.featured_image != blank
    assign og_img = page.featured_image
  elsif settings.share_image != blank
    assign og_img = settings.share_image
  elsif settings.featured_product.featured_image != blank
    assign og_img = settings.featured_product.featured_image
  else
    assign og_fallback_product = collections.all.products.first
    if og_fallback_product.featured_image != blank
      assign og_img = og_fallback_product.featured_image
    endif
  endif
-%}
{%- if og_img != blank -%}
  <meta property="og:image" content="https:{{ og_img | image_url: width: 1200 }}">
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ og_img.alt | default: doc_title | escape }}">
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Twitter/X reads og:* for title, description and image, so only the card
  type genuinely has to be declared. Declaring it is what upgrades the
  preview from a thumbnail beside a line of text to a full-width image card
  — and summary_large_image is only honest when an image actually resolved,
  which is why it is inside the same guard.
{%- endcomment -%}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="{% if og_img != blank %}summary_large_image{% else %}summary{% endif %}">
